{"maxId":318,"entries":{"chapter-65-study-major-depression|Chapter 65 Study: Major Depression":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|~5% of older adults meet criteria for major depression — lower than in other age groups; new-onset depression peaks in middle age; among older adults, greater age brings a similar likelihood of becoming depressed but a higher likelihood of remaining depressed (p1007).":["u3"],"copy|In primary care, ~5% of older adults have clinically significant depression; about half are recognized/diagnosed; of those, only 1 in 5 get guideline-concordant care — so only ~10% of depressed older adults get \"adequate\" care (p1012).":["u4"],"copy|Depression is not a normal part of aging; normalizing it in older patients is misguided and unproductive (p1007).":["u5"],"copy|PHQ-9 scoring: 0–4 = no/insignificant symptoms, 5–9 = mild/subsyndromal depression, ≥10 = typically correlates with major depression and warrants further evaluation and clinical intervention (p1009, p1012).":["u6"],"copy|Figure 65-2 combines symptom count, depressed mood or loss of interest, duration and functional impairment to guide diagnostic assessment. Its chronic-depression row specifies mood symptoms for two years. For standardized PHQ-9 severity, 10–14 is moderate, 15–19 moderately severe, and 20–27 severe (p1011, Kroenke et al.). The score supports evaluation and does not independently diagnose major depression.":["u7"],"copy|Item 9 asks about death wishes or more intensive suicidal ideation — clearly an important part of the assessment (p1012).":["u8"],"copy|Two core distinguishing features of geriatric depression: anhedonia and hopelessness — they may be important clues when deficits in basic needs (Maslow's hierarchy, Fig 65-1) are more apparent than depressed mood (p1008).":["u9"],"copy|Social isolation (objective — network size, contact frequency) vs loneliness (subjective — perceived inadequacy of connection) are distinct but closely related constructs; loneliness is fundamentally related to depression, whereas some isolation (living alone, selectivity) may reflect capability and preference and need not signify a problem (p1009).":["u10"],"copy|Suicide/safety checklist (Fig 65-3) is triggered by PHQ Q9 ≥1, MADRS Q10 ≥3, or QIDS-SR Q12 ≥1 (p1013).":["u11"],"copy|Structured assessment: suicidal thoughts, intent, plan, behavior; risk factors (hopelessness, impulsivity, isolation, prior attempts, recent psychiatric discharge, access to means) vs protective factors (reasons for living, supports); document firearm status and an action plan (p1013).":["u12"],"copy|Assess whether a plan is formulated/rehearsed and whether means (especially firearms) are accessible — removing access lowers immediate risk (p1018).":["u13"],"copy|Even in \"gold standard\" collaborative care: <50% of patients achieve a depression response (≥50% symptom reduction) at 6 months, <1/3 achieve remission — two-thirds remain effectively depressed; between-clinic response rates ranged from <10% to >80% (p1012).":["u14"],"copy|Largest geriatric depression treatment trial: 51% of patients preferred counseling/psychotherapy vs 38% preferring antidepressants (p1014).":["u15"],"copy|Structured psychotherapy trials run ~3 months; if no improvement by 1–2 months, consider switching modality (p1014).":["u16"],"copy|In studies summarized by Hazzard, ECT response exceeded 80% and remission exceeded 60%, with favorable responses in older adults (p1014). The APA describes an acute course commonly given 2–3 times weekly, often 6–12 sessions. Confusion and difficulty learning new material usually improve, but some autobiographical memory gaps may persist.Current practice (not the book): ECT is typically given two or three times per week; an acute course often consists of 6–12 treatments — American Psychiatric Association (patient page).":["u17"],"copy|Antidepressants do not show benefit for minor/subsyndromal depression despite it having similar health/QOL impact to major depression — don't reflexively medicate mild cases (p1012, p1015).":["u18"],"copy|Antidepressants do not generally work in dementia-associated depression; favor behavioral/caregiver-support interventions instead (p1015).":["u19"],"copy|Slow up-titration gave much better adherence than a higher starting dose, even though both reached the same eventual dose; separately, dose-response research does not support a minimum effective dosage or narrow therapeutic window (p1015).":["u20"],"copy|Patients often don't volunteer side effects (especially sexual dysfunction) unless asked directly (p1015).":["u21"],"copy|In some nursing home populations, almost half of residents are prescribed an antidepressant — far exceeding actual depression prevalence estimates (p1015).":["u22"],"copy|DSM-5 defines major depression as an \"episode\" — expected to improve over time even without treatment, which complicates decisions about indefinite maintenance therapy (p1015).":["u23"],"copy|Reduce antidepressants in steps, with smaller reductions as the dose falls. Hazzard describes 2–4 weeks for many SSRIs, SNRIs and mirtazapine and longer courses for TCAs (p1016). NICE recommends adjusting the pace to the drug, duration, prior withdrawal and symptoms. Some patients need weeks or months.":["u24"],"copy|Fluoxetine has a long half-life. NICE allows a period of alternate-day dosing for some people taking 20 mg/day and recommends gradual withdrawal at 40–60 mg/day. A clinician-directed switch from another drug requires attention to interactions and washout (p1016, NICE NG222 recommendation 1.4.18).Current practice (not the book): fluoxetine can sometimes be stopped by alternate-day dosing at 20 mg/day; at 40–60 mg/day use a gradual withdrawal schedule — NICE NG222 rec 1.4.18, 2022.":["u25"],"copy|Discontinuation symptoms (sweating, chills, dizziness, GI upset, vivid dreaming, vertigo, numbness, shock-like sensations) can be confused with relapse/lack of efficacy — misdiagnosis may lead to unnecessary tests, useless treatments, and increased costs (p1015–1016).":["u26"],"copy|Table 65-1 (p1017) catalogs challenges clinicians face treating depressed older patients — emotional toll (feeling drained, like a failure), practical friction (patients' \"pulling teeth\" pace, bottomless needs, family members wanting to explain at length what they've observed), and slow response (weeks to months) — the chapter frames \"caring for the caregiver\" (including the clinician) as part of good care (p1016).":["u27"],"copy|A suicidal patient introduces medicolegal concerns — protecting the patient's safety and ensuring the provider documents fully (Table 65-1, p1017).":["u28"],"copy|Simple human presence (sitting down, eye contact, asking \"what else is going on?\") is probably the most important way to ensure older patients can express themselves (p1017).":["u29"],"copy|Medically complex, cognitively impaired, or bipolar/psychotic-feature depression is best suited for geriatric psychiatry subspecialty referral; most general psychiatrists should feel comfortable evaluating and treating older adults (p1018–1019).":["u30"],"copy|ECT should be considered early for severe or psychotic depression, or after limited medication response (p1014).":["u31"],"copy|Subsyndromal depression has real health/QOL impact but antidepressants show no proven benefit for it — don't assume \"some symptoms = start a drug\" (p1012, p1015).":["u32"],"copy|No trial has found medications more effective than ECT, and older patients seem even more responsive to it than younger adults — contrary to its negative-press reputation (p1014).":["u33"],"copy|Fluoxetine is the one antidepressant that breaks the \"always taper slowly\" rule (p1016).":["u34"],"copy|The PHQ-9 diagnostic table's dysthymia row requires Q2 (depressed mood) specifically, sustained 2 years — not just any 3–4 symptoms (p1011).":["u35"],"recall|Recall":["u36"],"copy|CHAPTER 65s / HAZZARD":["u37"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 65 — Major Depression; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u38"],"copy|Epidemiology":["u39"],"copy|Screening & diagnosis (PHQ-9)":["u40"],"copy|Suicide risk assessment":["u41"],"copy|Real-world treatment efficacy":["u42"],"copy|Treatment options":["u43"],"copy|Stopping antidepressants":["u44"],"copy|Provider & caregiver factors":["u45"],"copy|Special populations & referral":["u46"],"copy|1.":["u48"],"copy|What percentage of older adults meet criteria for major depression, and how does that compare with other age groups?":["u49"],"copy|About 5% — lower than in other age groups; among older adults, greater age brings a similar likelihood of becoming depressed but a higher likelihood of remaining depressed (p1007).":["u50"],"copy|2.":["u51"],"copy|What PHQ-9 total score range is generally considered to indicate no or clinically insignificant depressive symptoms, and what range indicates mild/subsyndromal depression?":["u52"],"copy|0–4 = no/insignificant symptoms; 5–9 = mild, generally subsyndromal, depression (p1009, p1012).":["u53"],"copy|4.":["u54"],"copy|At what PHQ-9 score range is major depression classified as moderate, and at what range is it classified as severe?":["u55"],"copy|The validated PHQ-9 grades are 10–14 moderate, 15–19 moderately severe and 20–27 severe.":["u56"],"copy|Figure 65-2 prints “moderate” for 15–19 and “20” for severe, which differs from the validated severity bands (p1011, Kroenke et al.).":["u57"],"copy|5.":["u58"],"copy|What three screening-tool thresholds trigger the suicide/safety risk factor checklist?":["u59"],"copy|PHQ Question 9 ≥1, MADRS Question 10 ≥3, or QIDS-SR Question 12 ≥1 (p1013).":["u60"],"copy|6.":["u61"],"copy|In the largest treatment trial of geriatric depression to date, what proportion of patients preferred psychotherapy/counseling versus antidepressant medications?":["u62"],"copy|51% preferred counseling or psychotherapy; 38% preferred antidepressant medications (p1014).":["u63"],"copy|7.":["u64"],"copy|Even in \"gold standard\" collaborative care, what fraction of patients achieve a depression response at 6 months, and what fraction remain effectively depressed?":["u65"],"copy|Fewer than half achieve a depression response (≥50% symptom reduction); fewer than a third achieve remission — roughly two-thirds remain effectively depressed (p1012).":["u66"],"copy|8.":["u67"],"copy|What are the response and remission rates for ECT in treating depression, and how does its effectiveness compare with medications?":["u68"],"copy|Response rate >80%, remission rate >60% — no trial has ever found medications more effective than ECT, and older adults respond even better than younger adults (p1014).":["u69"],"copy|9.":["u70"],"copy|What is the typical initial ECT course (frequency and duration), and what happens to short-term memory loss?":["u71"],"copy|The APA describes two or three ECT treatments per week, often 6–12 treatments, individualized to response.":["u72"],"copy|Post-treatment confusion and difficulty retaining new information usually improve.":["u72"],"copy|Some memory gaps for events around the treatment period can persist.":["u72"],"copy|The chapter gives a different historical frequency (p1014).":["u72"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): ECT is typically given two or three times per week; an acute course often consists of 6–12 treatments — American Psychiatric Association (patient page).":["u73"],"copy|10.":["u74"],"copy|Do antidepressant medications show proven benefit for minor/subsyndromal depression, and how well do they generally work for depression in dementia?":["u75"],"copy|No clear antidepressant benefit for subsyndromal depression despite similar health impact to major depression, and antidepressants do not generally work for depression in the setting of dementia (p1012, p1015).":["u76"],"copy|12.":["u77"],"copy|Roughly what proportion of nursing home residents are prescribed an antidepressant, and how does that compare with estimated depression prevalence in that population?":["u78"],"copy|Almost half of residents in some nursing home populations are prescribed an antidepressant — far higher than any estimate of actual depression prevalence (p1015).":["u79"],"copy|13.":["u80"],"copy|How should antidepressant tapering be individualized, and how does fluoxetine's long half-life affect dose reduction?":["u81"],"copy|Reduce in steps guided by symptoms, half-life and duration of treatment.":["u82"],"copy|The book gives 2–4 weeks as an example for several agents and longer tapers for TCAs (p1016).":["u83"],"copy|NICE notes that 20 mg/day of fluoxetine can sometimes be reduced with alternate-day dosing, while 40–60 mg/day requires gradual reduction.":["u84"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): fluoxetine can sometimes be stopped by alternate-day dosing at 20 mg/day; at 40–60 mg/day use a gradual withdrawal schedule — NICE NG222 rec 1.4.18, 2022.":["u85"],"copy|14.":["u86"],"copy|When assessing a patient with suicidal ideation, what two specific factors most raise immediate risk, and what action reduces that risk?":["u87"],"copy|A specified, rehearsed plan and accessible means (especially firearms) most raise immediate risk; removing access to the means (eg, the firearm) decreases immediate risk (p1018).":["u88"],"copy|15.":["u89"],"copy|Which patients with depression are best suited for geriatric psychiatry subspecialty referral rather than general psychiatry?":["u90"],"copy|Medically complex or cognitively impaired patients, and those whose major depressive episode is related to bipolar disorder or has psychotic features (p1019).":["u91"],"copy|Answer":["u92","u93","u94","u95","u96","u97","u98","u99","u100","u101","u102","u103","u104"],"copy|In the studies summarized by Hazzard, ECT response exceeded 80% and remission exceeded 60%, with favorable responses in older adults. The average initial course was 3–6 treatments per week for 2–4 weeks. Short-term memory loss almost always resolved within a few weeks after the course (p1014).Current practice (not the book): ECT is commonly given 2–3 times weekly, often for 6–12 treatments. Some patients experience longer-lasting gaps in autobiographical memory — American Psychiatric Association, What Is Electroconvulsive Therapy?.":["u17"],"copy|Hazzard gives 2–4 weeks as a rough taper for most SSRIs, SNRIs and mirtazapine. It gives several months for tricyclics (p1016).":["u251"],"copy|Hazzard states that fluoxetine does not require tapering because of its long half-life. If withdrawal symptoms occur despite a long taper of an antidepressant, the chapter suggests considering a single 20 mg dose of fluoxetine (p1016).Current practice (not the book): reduce doses in stages, tailoring the pace to the medicine and symptoms. For fluoxetine 20 mg/day, alternate-day dosing can be a step down. At 40–60 mg/day, withdraw gradually — NICE NG222, recommendations 1.4.16–1.4.18, 2022.":["u252"],"copy|Discontinuation symptoms (sweating, chills, dizziness, flu-like symptoms, GI upset, insomnia, vivid and excessive dreaming, depressed mood, vertigo, numbness, shock-like sensations) can be confused with relapse/lack of efficacy — misdiagnosis may lead to unnecessary tests, useless treatments, and increased costs (p1015–1016).":["u26"],"copy|Hazzard gives an average initial course of 3–6 treatments per week for 2–4 weeks (p1014).":["u193"],"copy|Assess medical and anesthetic suitability.":["u198"],"copy|Hazzard says short-term memory loss almost always resolves within a few weeks after the course (p1014).":["u253"],"copy|The average initial course in Hazzard is 3–6 treatments per week for 2–4 weeks.":["u254"],"copy|Short-term memory loss almost always resolves within a few weeks after the course ends (p1014).":["u255"],"copy|Hazzard gives 2–4 weeks for most SSRIs, SNRIs and mirtazapine and several months for tricyclics.":["u83"],"copy|It states that fluoxetine does not require tapering.":["u256"],"copy|For symptoms despite a long taper, the chapter suggests considering one dose of fluoxetine 20 mg (p1016).":["u257"],"copy|CHAPTER 65 / HAZZARD":["u37"],"copy|Exam traps":["u47"],"major-depression|Major Depression":["u258"],"symptoms-screening-and-diagnosis|Symptoms, screening and diagnosis":["u259"],"reading-a-phq-9-when-the-score-and-the-story-differ|Reading a PHQ-9 when the score and the story differ":["u116"],"mild-symptoms-cognitive-complaints-and-major-depression|Mild symptoms, cognitive complaints and major depression":["u117"],"treatment-selection-and-response|Treatment selection and response":["u260"],"drug-effects-and-discontinuation|Drug effects and discontinuation":["u261"],"suicide-risk-support-and-referral|Suicide risk, support and referral":["u262"],"ect-counseling-and-continuation-treatment|ECT counseling and continuation treatment":["u120"],"suicide-risk-and-immediate-protection|Suicide risk and immediate protection":["u121"],"social-context-longitudinal-care-and-referral|Social context, longitudinal care and referral":["u122"],"key-numbers|Key numbers":["u263"],"israeli-context|Israeli context":["u264"],"test-yourself|Test yourself":["u265"],"copy|Interpret symptoms and impairment together.":["u266"],"copy|A screening score prompts assessment of severity, safety and the treatment the person can realistically sustain.":["u267"],"copy|The PHQ-9 asks about nine symptoms over two weeks.":["u124"],"copy|For severity, add the frequency points for all nine responses (0–3 each).":["u125"],"copy|For the provisional diagnosis in Figure 65-2, count questions 1–8 only when present on more than half the days or nearly every day.":["u126"],"copy|Count any endorsement of item 9 and ask about safety directly.":["u127"],"copy|A report of marked impairment on the separate functional question warrants investigation even when few symptoms meet the frequency threshold (p1009–1013).":["u128"],"copy|Ask which core symptom qualifies.":["u129"],"copy|Five or more qualifying symptoms including low mood or loss of interest, with impaired function, support evaluation for a major episode.":["u130"],"copy|The minor-depression row uses two to four qualifying symptoms with a core symptom and impairment.":["u131"],"copy|The chronic-depression row specifically requires low mood (item 2), a two-year history and impairment.":["u132"],"copy|Severity scores and diagnostic counts serve different purposes.":["u133"],"copy|A total of 10 or more generally warrants assessment for major depression, and following the score over visits can reveal which symptoms have improved (p1010–1012).":["u134"],"copy|Figure 65-2 combines symptom count, depressed mood or loss of interest, duration and functional impairment to guide diagnostic assessment.":["u268"],"copy|Its chronic-depression row specifies mood symptoms for two years.":["u269"],"copy|For standardized PHQ-9 severity, 10–14 is moderate, 15–19 moderately severe, and 20–27 severe (p1011, Kroenke et al.).":["u270"],"copy|The score supports evaluation and does not independently diagnose major depression.":["u271"],"copy|In May 2024 Q16, reduced interest, fatigue and appetite change each occurred on several days.":["u135"],"copy|Total 3, zero qualifying symptoms, daily tasks very difficult.":["u136"],"copy|In June 2025 Q57, interest, mood, energy and appetite each scored one.":["u137"],"copy|Total 4, again zero qualifying symptoms and marked interference.":["u138"],"copy|Both exams credited minor depression as well as a response compatible with the low count.":["u139"],"copy|In clinic, explore impaired function, sleep, illness, medication and distress, and arrange follow-up.":["u140"],"copy|Neither image meets the printed minor-depression algorithm (p1011 Figure 65-2).":["u141"],"copy|Ask what the person has stopped enjoying and why.":["u142"],"copy|Apathy, pain, illness and isolation can all reduce activity.":["u143"],"copy|Loss of pleasure and hopelessness can interfere with eating, obtaining essentials and sustaining relationships.":["u144"],"copy|Compare mood, cognition and function with the prior baseline and listen to the patient as well as the family.":["u145"],"copy|In June 2022 Q19, new concentration and recall problems after bereavement and a change from MMSE 24 to 28 are compatible with a depressive contribution.":["u146"],"copy|Neither improvement nor a single test identifies the medication taken or excludes later cognitive disease (p1008–1012).":["u147"],"copy|In one presentation, crying followed a loss of family authority, while enjoyment of grandchildren and the day center continued.":["u272"],"copy|A PHQ-9 of 5, no reported suicidality and a preference against medication favor a conversation about her role, activities she values, and planned follow-up.":["u149"],"copy|Distress deserves help even when it falls below a major episode.":["u150"],"copy|In dementia, combine a careful review of medical contributors with caregiver support and realistic activities.":["u151"],"copy|Complex depression with cognitive impairment is a reason to involve geriatric mental health expertise (p1012, 1014–1015, 1018–1019).":["u152"],"copy|Two weeks of yearning after a spouse’s death can occur in acute grief.":["u153"],"copy|Ask whether self-worth and the capacity to enjoy contact are preserved or whether worthlessness, pervasive anhedonia, hopelessness or suicidal thoughts suggest an additional depressive episode.":["u154"],"copy|Female sex is associated with vulnerability to prolonged grief.":["u273"],"copy|It does not ask for a diagnosis after two weeks.":["u156"],"copy|In adults DSM-5-TR requires at least 12 months after the death for prolonged grief disorder (ch66 p1024; American Psychiatric Association, Prolonged Grief Disorder).":["u157"],"copy|Pitfall.":["u274","u278","u279","u290"],"copy|Begin with the indication.":["u158"],"copy|People with major depression may choose a drug, structured psychotherapy or another suitable intervention.":["u159"],"copy|Trials in mild or subsyndromal depression show little evidence of a drug benefit.":["u160"],"copy|In depression with dementia, check for a measurable improvement before adding to long-term polypharmacy.":["u161"],"copy|Placebo-group improvement can be considerable, especially in less severe episodes.":["u162"],"copy|Improvement after a prescription can reflect time, clinical contact, expectation and a drug effect.":["u163"],"copy|The book cites about six to eight weeks for a measurable antidepressant effect.":["u164"],"copy|Continue to check symptoms and safety during that interval (p1012, 1014–1015).":["u165"],"copy|Review adherence, time on treatment, adverse effects, interacting medicines and the diagnosis before calling a drug ineffective.":["u166"],"copy|Sertraline 50 mg had been tolerated for months without benefit.":["u275"],"copy|A gradual trial toward 100 mg with reassessment was reasonable.":["u168"],"copy|Two weeks at escitalopram 5 mg had produced some improvement.":["u276"],"copy|If tolerated, a move to 10 mg allows a fuller trial before adding a second medicine.":["u170"],"copy|Neither dose is a universal therapeutic threshold.":["u171"],"copy|A lower initial dose and planned reviews can improve adherence even if the eventual dose is the same.":["u172"],"copy|Ask explicitly about sexual effects because patients may otherwise stop treatment without explaining why (p929 Table 60-9, p1014–1015).":["u173"],"copy|June 2026 Q87 concerns depression in later life.":["u174"],"copy|The printed pages 114–115 concern a different chapter.":["u175"],"copy|The placebo discussion on p1014 and the individualized-dose paragraph on p1015 supply the depression teaching.":["u176"],"copy|Benefit in dementia is uncertain, and response within placebo groups can be substantial (p1014–1015).":["u277"],"copy|Match the adverse effect to the agent.":["u178"],"copy|Paroxetine adds anticholinergic burden in someone with memory impairment, delirium risk or impaired bladder emptying.":["u179"],"copy|Citalopram has less anticholinergic activity but can prolong QT in a dose-related fashion.":["u180"],"copy|In a patient taking citalopram, the ECG shows wide-complex tachycardia with changing amplitude, making citalopram the important association.":["u280"],"copy|Both citalopram and escitalopram can cause SIADH.":["u182"],"copy|Tramadol plus venlafaxine raises concern for serotonin toxicity because both enhance serotonergic signaling.":["u281"],"copy|Both delirium with paroxetine and serotonin toxicity with citalopram are possible.":["u282"],"copy|Its published comparison is chapter 60, Table 60-9, alongside the Beers Criteria (p929; FDA prescribing information, citalopram and venlafaxine).":["u185"],"copy|Ask what will happen if the drug helps, and when it should be reviewed if it does not.":["u186"],"copy|Repeated episodes or clear recurrence following prior discontinuation favor longer treatment.":["u187"],"copy|Review benefit, falls, hyponatremia and other adverse effects even after years of stability.":["u188"],"copy|Taper in steps when discontinuation is appropriate and assess symptoms as the dose falls.":["u189"],"copy|Vivid dreams, insomnia, vertigo, gastrointestinal upset or sensory disturbances emerging after a reduction suggest withdrawal.":["u190"],"copy|Compare their timing and character with the previous depressive episode before deciding to restart long-term therapy (p1015–1016).":["u191"],"copy|Hazzard gives 2–4 weeks as a rough taper for most SSRIs, SNRIs and mirtazapine.":["u283"],"copy|It gives several months for tricyclics (p1016).":["u284"],"copy|Hazzard states that fluoxetine does not require tapering because of its long half-life.":["u285"],"copy|If withdrawal symptoms occur despite a long taper of an antidepressant, the chapter suggests considering a single 20 mg dose of fluoxetine (p1016).":["u286"],"copy|Current practice: Individualized antidepressant taper. reduce doses in stages, tailoring the pace to the medicine and symptoms.":["u287"],"copy|For fluoxetine 20 mg/day, alternate-day dosing can be a step down.":["u288"],"copy|At 40–60 mg/day, withdraw gradually — NICE NG222, recommendations 1.4.16–1.4.18, 2022.":["u289"],"copy|For a severely depressed patient losing weight, becoming psychotic or failing adequate medication trials, discuss ECT early.":["u192"],"copy|Response means substantial clinical improvement, whereas remission means few or no remaining symptoms.":["u194"],"copy|In most studies summarized here, over 80% responded and over 60% remitted.":["u195"],"copy|Older adults often responded particularly well.":["u196"],"copy|Current practice: ECT frequency and duration.":["u291"],"copy|ECT is commonly given 2–3 times weekly, often for 6–12 treatments.":["u292"],"copy|Some patients experience longer-lasting gaps in autobiographical memory — American Psychiatric Association, What Is Electroconvulsive Therapy?.":["u293"],"copy|Plan beyond the acute response because benefit can diminish, even during medication treatment.":["u200"],"copy|Follow symptoms and discuss continuation medication or maintenance ECT where appropriate.":["u201"],"copy|For patients who prefer psychotherapy, offer an accessible structured intervention with explicit goals.":["u202"],"copy|The chapter describes cognitive behavioral, interpersonal and group approaches, including reminiscence.":["u203"],"copy|Reconsider the chosen modality if there is no improvement after roughly one to two months (p1014).":["u204"],"copy|Ask a caregiver with insomnia about exhaustion and suicidal thinking.":["u294"],"copy|Figure 65-3 lists insomnia and caregiving stress among the possible risks.":["u206"],"copy|It also lists reasons for living, supports and responsibility to others as possible protective factors.":["u207"],"copy|Ask what caring for her spouse means to her, how she sleeps and who can give her respite.":["u208"],"copy|A protective factor does not cancel an expression of suicidal intent.":["u209"],"copy|Clinical context appears on p1017 and risk assessment on p1018; the two examined factors appear together on the p1013 checklist (p1013, 1017–1018).":["u210"],"copy|Ask directly about thoughts, intent, a plan, prior behavior and access to means.":["u211"],"copy|A wish to die may express unbearable pain, hopelessness, a desire for contact or imminent intent.":["u212"],"copy|Take the statement seriously and explore it during this encounter.":["u213"],"copy|Seek collateral information, reduce access to lethal means with appropriate support and arrange emergency evaluation for imminent danger.":["u214"],"copy|Treat pain and isolation at the same time.":["u215"],"copy|In an older person with cancer, more severe pain and depressive symptoms can coexist.":["u216"],"copy|A discussion of care goals and symptom relief can proceed alongside the safety assessment (p1013, 1017–1018).":["u217"],"copy|Simple human presence (sitting down, eye contact, asking \"what else is going on?\")":["u295"],"copy|is probably the most important way to ensure older patients can express themselves (p1017).":["u296"],"copy|Depression is less prevalent in later life than in some younger age groups, yet an older person who becomes depressed may remain so longer.":["u218"],"copy|A loss of role, function or connection can be clinically important without meeting criteria for major depression.":["u219"],"copy|Ask about basic needs, security, belonging and what still feels worthwhile.":["u220"],"copy|Depression and disability can reinforce one another when poor motivation makes self-care harder and worsening illness restricts participation (p1007–1009).":["u221"],"copy|Living alone describes a social arrangement.":["u222"],"copy|Loneliness describes how well a person’s relationships meet their needs.":["u223"],"copy|Someone may choose solitude and feel connected, or be lonely despite frequent contact.":["u224"],"copy|One practical approach is Act–Belong–Commit: identify something the patient can enjoy, someone to do it with and a meaningful role worth sustaining.":["u225"],"copy|Physical activity is associated with lower onset or recurrence, and a realistic activity plan can support health alongside depression treatment (p1009, 1014).":["u226"],"copy|Collaborative care uses a designated care manager for contact and symptom tracking, a prescribing primary clinician and psychiatric consultation.":["u227"],"copy|Response and remission remain distinct: in real-world collaborative care, fewer than half achieved a 50% symptom reduction at six months and fewer than one-third remitted.":["u228"],"copy|Selected trial participants, intensive check-ins and short follow-up can make research outcomes look better than routine practice.":["u229"],"copy|Agree on what improvement should look like and make time to review it (p1012, 1014).":["u230"],"copy|During follow-up, ask about stressors and arrange practical help, respite or a call between visits.":["u231"],"copy|Clinicians may feel drained when responses are slow or the family’s concerns seem endless.":["u232"],"copy|Reflecting with a colleague can preserve the capacity to listen.":["u233"],"copy|In long-term care, offer choices, reduce unwanted noise, identify valued activities and consider reminiscence work before escalating drug treatment.":["u234"],"copy|Palliative discussions can address pain, social and spiritual support, and what quality of life means throughout serious illness.":["u235"],"copy|Worsening symptoms, psychosis, bipolar features, complex drug combinations or consideration of ECT justify psychiatric referral (p1016–1019).":["u236"],"copy|בבירור ירידה תפקודית כדאי לשאול על שינוי בשינה ובתיאבון, על הנאה מקשרים חברתיים ועל ירידה בזיכרון ובריכוז.":["u237"],"copy|משרד הבריאות מציין שלעיתים התלונה הבולטת בגיל המבוגר היא דווקא גופנית.":["u238"],"copy|אם השינוי הופיע לאחר התחלת תרופה, מומלץ לברר אותו עם רופא המשפחה ולהביא את רשימת התרופות.":["u239"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, חרדה ודיכאון בגיל השלישי, 20.11.2025, הסעיף ״איך מאבחנים דיכאון״.":["u240"],"copy|אפשר לקבל טיפול קוגניטיבי־התנהגותי במסגרת קופת החולים.":["u241"],"copy|בעת טיפול תרופתי משרד הבריאות מדגיש מעקב אחר הפרעות במלחים ובדיקת שילובים עם תרופות קבועות.":["u242"],"copy|בלבול חדש לאחר התחלת טיפול הוא סיבה לפנות לבדיקה ולציין אילו תרופות ניטלות.":["u243"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, חרדה ודיכאון בגיל השלישי, ״טיפול בדיכאון ובחרדה בגיל השלישי״ ו״למה חשוב לשים לב אצל מבוגרים עם דמנציה ודיכאון״.":["u244"],"copy|כאשר תסמיני מצב רוח וירידה קוגניטיבית מופיעים יחד, רופא המשפחה יכול לסייע בבחירת הייעוץ המתאים.":["u245"],"copy|כדאי לברר עם הקופה אם נדרשת הפניה.":["u246"],"copy|האבחון הפסיכוגריאטרי המתואר כולל הערכה קוגניטיבית, הערכת הפרעות נפשיות ובדיקות גופניות לפי הצורך.":["u247"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, פסיכוגריאטריה, 11.10.2023, הסעיפים ״מתי לפנות לגריאטר ומתי לפסיכוגריאטר״ ו״תהליך אבחון פסיכוגריאטרי״.":["u248"],"copy|2020 Q65 Titrating sertraline p929":["u297"],"copy|June 2022 Q41 Citalopram and QT interval p929":["u298"],"copy|June 2022 Q52 Escitalopram titration p929":["u299"],"copy|June 2022 Q88 Comparing anticholinergic burden p929":["u300"],"copy|September 2024 Q94 Paroxetine and delirium p929":["u301"],"copy|December 2021 Q79 Pain and depression in cancer p1008":["u302"],"copy|June 2022 Q19 Depression with cognitive symptoms p1008":["u303"],"copy|June 2022 Q47 Risk of prolonged grief p1008":["u304"],"copy|2020 Q37 Interpreting PHQ-9 p1009":["u305"],"copy|May 2024 Q16 Minor versus major depression p1011":["u306"],"copy|June 2025 Q57 Monitoring minor depression p1011":["u307"],"copy|May 2024 Q39 Antidepressant efficacy in dementia p1014":["u308"],"copy|June 2025 Q69 Placebo response in trials p1014":["u309"],"copy|June 2026 Q13 Non-drug treatment in dementia p1012":["u310"],"copy|2020 Q44 Serotonergic drug interactions p1014":["u311"],"copy|June 2026 Q87 Explaining antidepressant benefit p1014":["u312"],"copy|June 2023 Q65 Sleep and suicide risk p1013":["u313"],"copy|May 2024 Q30 Counseling about ECT remission p1014":["u314"],"copy|June 2025 Q31 ECT safety in later life p1014":["u315"],"copy|Current practice note":["u316","u318"],"c